BT /F2 8 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 755.00 Tm (THE BIG MONEY PLAYBOOKS) Tj ET
0.6 w 54.00 747.00 m 558.00 747.00 l S
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 716.00 Tm (The first split is the classic one, time slicing versus MIG, and the practitioners do not land in the same place. Emmanuel) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 700.77 Tm (Corels Academy ran the same jobs shared and then on separate MIG instances on an A100, with a distinct) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 685.55 Tm (CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES per job, and at 50 users throughput moved from 0.76 to 1.0 requests per second per GPU,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 670.32 Tm (yet mean speech latency came in lower with time sharing [sRtvITK_o8I @ 00:03:35]. So the answer depends on) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 655.10 Tm (whether a tenant is buying throughput or responsiveness. Julien Semaan of Kubex concedes in Q&A that MIG is) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 639.87 Tm (usually faster but static, and his entire build targets making time slicing memory-safe rather than abandoning it) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 624.65 Tm ([Vw7DTM1SV60 @ 00:20:40]. Kevin Klues frames it as a fundamental inherent trade-off: MIG buys full fault) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 609.42 Tm (isolation and memory-bandwidth QoS but locks you into fixed partition sizes, while time partitioning gives flexibility) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 594.20 Tm (that you pay for in contention [nOgxv_R13Dg @ 00:05:00]. The Code Architect settles the fight by workload class) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 578.97 Tm (instead of ideology: time slicing for bursty heterogeneous workloads, MPS for trusted and predictable ones, and MIG) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 563.75 Tm (only when you need absolute isolation [TwICQ_zP8Ys @ 00:08:03]. The operator read is that no single sharing mode) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 548.52 Tm (wins every tenant mix, so each tenant gets placed and priced by the load profile it actually runs, not by whichever mode) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 533.30 Tm (the platform team installed first.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 518.07 Tm (Fractional GPUs split the field a second time, over whether a fraction is a promise or an enforced wall. Saiyam and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 502.85 Tm (Mike Peterson at vCluster demo the KAI approach: annotate the pod with gpu-fraction 0.5 while keeping the limit at 1) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 487.62 Tm ([sXpJ4vSc-cM @ 00:38:38]. Semaan warns that this controls placement only; KAI does not enforce the fraction at) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 472.40 Tm (runtime, so noisy neighbors remain possible, and his fix layers HAMi-core underneath, intercepting CUDA calls) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 457.17 Tm (through LD_PRELOAD so a 0.35 fraction becomes an 8,061 MB CUDA_DEVICE_MEMORY_LIMIT) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 441.95 Tm ([Vw7DTM1SV60 @ 00:10:30]. The Code Architect hits the same wall from the Ray side: a 0.25 GPU request can still) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 426.72 Tm (saturate the whole device because Ray only sets CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES, so the fix lives in the application, tuning) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 411.50 Tm (vLLM's GPU memory utilization to match the Ray placement fraction [TwICQ_zP8Ys @ 00:06:22]. Yuan Chen's) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 396.27 Tm (benchmarks push a third playbook, MPS with explicit limits: capping a low-priority job at 10% gave the other job) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 381.05 Tm (near-dedicated performance under heavy load, where plain time slicing pinned the GPU at 100% and degraded badly) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 365.82 Tm ([nOgxv_R13Dg @ 00:20:45]. Semaan's own talk counters that MPS adds a daemon that becomes another point of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 350.60 Tm (failure. The real choice is where enforcement lives: in the CUDA interception layer, in each application's memory) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 335.37 Tm (config, or in an extra daemon on every GPU node.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 320.15 Tm (The tenancy wrapper is the third fault line. vCluster's Saiyam and Mike Peterson argue namespaces provide weak) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 304.92 Tm (isolation, you can't do CRDs, and 3,000 secrets in one namespace load etcd for everyone, so each tenant gets its own) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 289.70 Tm (virtual cluster, with the host either syncing node information in or exposing only a runtime class [sXpJ4vSc-cM @) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 274.47 Tm (00:41:50]. Core Concept Learning's Run:ai playbook stays in one shared cluster and runs it like a quota market:) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 259.25 Tm (departments and projects hold guaranteed GPU quotas, research borrows analytics' idle GPUs overnight, and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 244.02 Tm (preemption claws the quota back when the owner returns [56JbSXiXhhE @ 00:00:56]. Luca Berton's OpenShift AI) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 228.80 Tm (build for a French avionics customer is governance-heavy: tenants arrive through PR-based templates covering RBAC,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 213.57 Tm (network policies and quotas, while KAI caps, priority classes and preemption keep interactive work above training) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 198.35 Tm ([nABVTFBmV2M @ 00:13:02]. SUSE moves the boundary down to the virtual machine, carving MIG-backed vGPUs) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 183.12 Tm (in Harvester into 1G.5GB, 2G.10GB and 3G.20GB profiles and attaching each partition to its own VM, with both VMs) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 167.90 Tm (seeing the GPU [4q3LHacRheg @ 00:00:48]. Each camp trades operational weight for a harder boundary: borrowing is) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 152.67 Tm (light and fast, PR-gated templates leave an audit trail, and VM-attached partitions give the hardest line of the four. The) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 137.45 Tm (operator picks the wrapper first, then picks the slicing mode that runs underneath it.) Tj ET
